  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">MONITORING AND PROTECTING THE GREATER NOUABALE-NDOKI LANDSCAPE OF CONGO-2</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">THE PURPOSE OF THIS PROJECT IS TO SUPPORT AN ELEPHANT CONSERVATION EFFORT IN NOUABALE-NDOKI NATIONAL PARK (NNNP) AND ADJACENT FORESTRY CONCESSIONS BY IMPROVING BIOLOGICAL AND HUMAN DISTURBANCE MONITORING AND BUILDING THE CAPACITY OF CENTRAL AFRICAN CONSERVATIONISTS. NORTHERN REPUBLIC OF CONGO, AS PART OF THE GREATER SANGHA-NDOKI REGION, IS ONE OF THE LAST REMAINING FOREST ELEPHANT STRONGHOLDS, WITH A POPULATION ESTIMATE OF MORE THAN 7,000 ELEPHANTS IN NNNP AND ITS PERIPHERY. THIS PROJECT IS INTENDED TO CONSERVE AFRICAN FOREST ELEPHANTS AND THEIR HABITAT BY ADDRESSING THE IMPACTS OF POACHING AND SUPPORTING EVIDENCE-BASED DECISION MAKING BY CONSERVATION MANAGERS. SPECIFIC ACTIVITIES INCLUDE: (1) MAINTAINING A LANDSCAPE SCALE ACOUSTIC MONITORING SYSTEM AND ASSOCIATED DATA MANAGEMENT AND ANALYSES (2) MONITORING OF ELEPHANT POPULATION DYNAMICS AND HABITAT USE, IN ADDITION TO ILLEGAL HUNTING WITH IMPROVED ACCURACY VIA CAMERA TRAPS AND (3) ESTABLISHING A CENTER FOR ANALYSIS AND TRAINING AT NNNP TO SUPPORT CENTRAL AFRICAN BIOLOGISTS AND CONSERVATIONISTS.</narrative>
  </description>
